CAT = "pllekInternal"; SEG = "INT"
Q = [
('A 68-year-old with new atrial fibrillation has a CHA2DS2-VA score of 3 and normal renal function. Best stroke prevention?',
 'Direct oral anticoagulant', ['Low-dose aspirin alone', 'Aspirin with clopidogrel', 'No antithrombotic drug'],
 'The ESC 2024 atrial fibrillation guideline recommends oral anticoagulation at a score of 2 or more, preferring a DOAC over a vitamin K antagonist. Antiplatelet therapy is not an alternative for stroke prevention.'),
('Chest pain for 2 hours, ST elevation in V1 to V4, and a catheter lab 40 minutes away. Reperfusion strategy?',
 'Primary PCI', ['Fibrinolysis in the ambulance', 'Angiography next week', 'Heparin infusion, no reperfusion'],
 'Primary PCI is preferred when it can be done within 120 minutes of the STEMI diagnosis. Fibrinolysis is the fallback only when that delay cannot be met.'),
('A patient with HFrEF remains symptomatic on ramipril. Which switch further lowers mortality?',
 'Sacubitril/valsartan', ['Aliskiren', 'Losartan plus ramipril', 'Ivabradine'],
 'Replacing the ACE inhibitor with sacubitril/valsartan cut cardiovascular death in PARADIGM-HF. Combining an ACE inhibitor with an ARB or aliskiren adds harm, and ivabradine lowers admissions only.'),
('Potassium is 7.1 mmol/l with ECG changes and IV calcium has been given. Which treatment shifts potassium into cells fastest?',
 'IV insulin with glucose', ['Oral patiromer', 'IV furosemide', 'Repeat IV calcium'],
 'Insulin with glucose lowers potassium within 15 to 30 minutes; nebulised salbutamol adds to it. Calcium protects the heart but does not lower potassium, and binders and diuretics are slow.'),
('Hypotension, hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia and skin hyperpigmentation suggest adrenal crisis. Immediate treatment?',
 'IV hydrocortisone', ['Oral fludrocortisone', 'Oral levothyroxine', 'IV insulin with glucose'],
 'Adrenal crisis needs hydrocortisone at once, usually 100 mg IV, with saline. Fludrocortisone is a later oral maintenance drug and is not needed while hydrocortisone doses are high.'),
('Diabetic ketoacidosis with potassium 3.1 mmol/l. What must happen before the insulin infusion starts?',
 'Replace potassium first', ['Give IV bicarbonate', 'Give an insulin bolus', 'Stop all IV fluids'],
 'Insulin drives potassium into cells, so a potassium below about 3.3 mmol/l is corrected before insulin to avoid dangerous hypokalaemia and arrhythmia. Fluids continue throughout.'),
('A man with type 2 diabetes and prior myocardial infarction takes metformin. Which add-on has proven cardiovascular benefit?',
 'GLP-1 receptor agonist', ['Sulfonylurea, gliclazide', 'DPP-4 inhibitor, sitagliptin', 'Basal insulin glargine'],
 'GLP-1 receptor agonists such as semaglutide and liraglutide reduced major cardiovascular events in outcome trials. Sulfonylureas, DPP-4 inhibitors and insulin were neutral at best.'),
('Fasting plasma glucose threshold that diagnoses diabetes when found on two occasions:',
 '126 mg/dl', ['100 mg/dl', '140 mg/dl', '200 mg/dl'],
 'Fasting glucose of 126 mg/dl (7.0 mmol/l) or more on two occasions diagnoses diabetes. 100 to 125 mg/dl is impaired fasting glucose, and 200 mg/dl applies to random or 2-hour OGTT values.'),
('A woman has thyrotoxicosis, a diffuse goitre and exophthalmos. Which antibody confirms the likely cause?',
 'TSH receptor antibodies', ['Anti-thyroglobulin antibodies', 'Anti-GAD antibodies', 'Antimitochondrial antibodies'],
 'Graves disease is driven by stimulating TSH receptor antibodies (TRAb). Anti-GAD points to autoimmune diabetes and antimitochondrial antibodies to primary biliary cholangitis.'),
('A 79-year-old with stable angina is newly diagnosed with hypothyroidism. How should levothyroxine be started?',
 'Low dose, titrated slowly', ['Full weight-based dose', 'Liothyronine loading dose', 'Weekly high-dose tablet'],
 'In older patients with coronary disease, starting at 12.5 to 25 micrograms and raising slowly avoids provoking angina or arrhythmia. A full replacement dose suits young, healthy adults.'),
('Which arterial blood gas finding qualifies a stable COPD patient for long-term oxygen therapy?',
 'PaO2 at or below 55 mmHg', ['SpO2 below 94% on exertion', 'PaCO2 above 45 mmHg', 'FEV1 below 50% predicted'],
 'Long-term oxygen is indicated when resting PaO2 is 55 mmHg (7.3 kPa) or less, or 55 to 60 mmHg with cor pulmonale or polycythaemia. Hypercapnia or a low FEV1 alone does not qualify.'),
('Preferred reliever for an adult with mild asthma under current GINA guidance:',
 'As-needed ICS-formoterol', ['As-needed SABA alone', 'Daily oral montelukast', 'Daily oral prednisolone'],
 'GINA advises against SABA-only treatment because it raises the risk of severe attacks. Low-dose budesonide-formoterol as needed is the preferred track.'),
('In community-acquired pneumonia, which finding scores a point in CURB-65?',
 'Urea above 7 mmol/l', ['Heart rate above 100', 'Temperature above 39 C', 'SpO2 below 92%'],
 'CURB-65 scores confusion, urea over 7 mmol/l, respiratory rate 30 or more, low blood pressure and age 65 or over. Pulse, fever and saturation are not part of it.'),
('CT confirms pulmonary embolism in a patient with systolic pressure 75 mmHg. Treatment of choice?',
 'Systemic thrombolysis', ['Oral apixaban', 'IVC filter first', 'Prophylactic heparin dose'],
 'High-risk PE with shock needs reperfusion, usually systemic thrombolysis with unfractionated heparin. A filter is reserved for those who cannot be anticoagulated.'),
('Suspected pulmonary embolism with low clinical probability on the Wells score. Next step?',
 'D-dimer test', ['CT pulmonary angiography', 'Empirical thrombolysis', 'Lower limb venography'],
 'With low or intermediate probability, a negative D-dimer safely rules out PE. CT angiography follows a positive D-dimer or a high clinical probability.'),
('A man with cirrhosis vomits blood and varices are suspected. Before endoscopy, which treatment matters most?',
 'Terlipressin and antibiotic', ['Oral propranolol and PPI', 'Fresh frozen plasma alone', 'IV tranexamic acid'],
 'Terlipressin or somatostatin plus antibiotic prophylaxis, started before endoscopy, lower rebleeding and death. Beta-blockers are for prevention, and tranexamic acid gave no benefit in GI bleeding.'),
('First-line Helicobacter pylori eradication in Poland, where clarithromycin resistance is high:',
 'Bismuth quadruple, 14 days', ['Clarithromycin triple, 7 days', 'PPI for 8 weeks', 'Amoxicillin for 5 days'],
 'Where clarithromycin resistance exceeds 15%, Maastricht VI and Polish guidance favour 14-day bismuth quadruple therapy: PPI, bismuth, tetracycline and metronidazole.'),
('Which test screens for coeliac disease in an adult still eating gluten?',
 'Anti-tTG IgA with total IgA', ['Faecal calprotectin level', 'Anti-Saccharomyces antibodies', 'HLA-B27 genotyping'],
 'Tissue transglutaminase IgA is the screening test, with total IgA to detect IgA deficiency, which gives false negatives. The diet must contain gluten for the result to be valid.'),
('Which feature favours Crohn disease over ulcerative colitis?',
 'Skip lesions with fistulae', ['Continuous rectal involvement', 'Inflammation limited to mucosa', 'Bloody diarrhoea'],
 'Crohn disease is transmural and patchy, so skip lesions, fistulae and strictures are typical. Ulcerative colitis starts in the rectum and spreads continuously within the mucosa.'),
('Which scoring system grades the severity of acute pancreatitis using results over the first 48 hours?',
 'Glasgow-Imrie criteria', ['CURB-65 score', 'Child-Pugh score', 'Wells score'],
 'The Glasgow-Imrie criteria score age, white cells, glucose, urea, PaO2, calcium, albumin and LDH; 3 or more predicts severe pancreatitis. Child-Pugh grades cirrhosis.'),
('Hepatic encephalopathy recurs in a cirrhotic patient despite regular lactulose. Which drug is added?',
 'Rifaximin', ['Long-term neomycin', 'Lorazepam', 'Loperamide'],
 'Rifaximin added to lactulose prevents recurrent overt hepatic encephalopathy. Sedatives and constipating drugs precipitate it.'),
('Which urinary finding suggests prerenal acute kidney injury rather than tubular necrosis?',
 'FENa below 1%', ['Muddy brown granular casts', 'Urine osmolality near 300', 'Red cell casts'],
 'Intact tubules conserve sodium in prerenal states, so fractional sodium excretion is under 1%. Muddy brown casts point to acute tubular necrosis and red cell casts to glomerulonephritis.'),
('Which finding defines nephrotic syndrome in an adult?',
 'Proteinuria above 3.5 g/day', ['Haematuria with red cell casts', 'Creatinine doubling in a week', 'Proteinuria of 0.5 g/day'],
 'Nephrotic syndrome is heavy proteinuria (over 3.5 g a day) with hypoalbuminaemia and oedema, often with hyperlipidaemia. Red cell casts indicate a nephritic picture.'),
('A patient on metformin now has a stable eGFR of 25 ml/min/1.73 m2. What should happen to metformin?',
 'Stop it', ['Double the dose', 'Keep the same dose', 'Switch to slow release'],
 'Metformin is contraindicated below an eGFR of 30 because of the risk of lactic acidosis. Between 30 and 45 the dose is reduced.'),
('Maximum safe rise in serum sodium during the first day of treating chronic hyponatraemia:',
 '10 mmol/l in 24 hours', ['20 mmol/l in 24 hours', '5 mmol/l per hour', '15 mmol/l in 6 hours'],
 'European guidance limits the rise to 10 mmol/l in the first 24 hours, and lower in high-risk patients. Faster correction risks osmotic demyelination.'),
('A 65-year-old man has new iron deficiency anaemia with no obvious bleeding. Next step?',
 'Gastroscopy and colonoscopy', ['Oral iron, recheck in a year', 'Bone marrow biopsy', 'Haemoglobin electrophoresis'],
 'Iron deficiency in a man or postmenopausal woman needs investigation of the upper and lower GI tract for cancer. Iron alone could mask a tumour.'),
('Macrocytic anaemia with reduced vibration sense in the feet. Which antibody suggests pernicious anaemia?',
 'Anti-intrinsic factor', ['Anti-dsDNA antibodies', 'Anti-tTG IgA', 'Anti-CCP antibodies'],
 'Intrinsic factor antibodies are specific for pernicious anaemia, the autoimmune cause of B12 malabsorption. Treatment is lifelong parenteral hydroxocobalamin.'),
('Lymph node biopsy in a young adult with painless cervical nodes shows large binucleate cells with owl-eye nucleoli. Diagnosis?',
 'Hodgkin lymphoma', ['Burkitt lymphoma', 'Cat scratch disease', 'Tuberculous adenitis'],
 'Owl-eye Reed-Sternberg cells are the hallmark of Hodgkin lymphoma. Burkitt lymphoma shows a starry sky pattern instead.'),
('A 74-year-old has lymphocytosis of 40 G/l and smudge cells on the blood film. Likely diagnosis?',
 'Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia', ['Acute lymphoblastic leukaemia', 'Hairy cell leukaemia', 'Infectious mononucleosis'],
 'Persistent mature B-cell lymphocytosis with smudge cells in an older adult is typical of CLL, confirmed by flow cytometry. Many patients need only monitoring at first.'),
('Before chemotherapy for a bulky high-grade lymphoma, how is tumour lysis syndrome prevented?',
 'Hydration and rasburicase', ['Potassium supplements', 'Calcium gluconate infusion', 'Strict fluid restriction'],
 'High-risk patients get vigorous hydration with rasburicase or allopurinol to limit uric acid. Potassium and phosphate rise in tumour lysis, so supplements are avoided.'),
('Calcium 3.6 mmol/l in a woman with metastatic breast cancer. Initial treatment?',
 'Saline, then zoledronate', ['Hydrochlorothiazide', 'Oral vitamin D3', 'Furosemide without fluids'],
 'Severe hypercalcaemia of malignancy is treated with rehydration and then an IV bisphosphonate, or denosumab. Thiazides raise calcium and must be stopped.'),
('Facial swelling, distended neck veins and dyspnoea in a heavy smoker. Most common cause of this syndrome?',
 'Lung cancer', ['Thyroid goitre', 'Constrictive pericarditis', 'Breast cancer'],
 'Superior vena cava obstruction is most often caused by lung cancer, especially small cell, followed by lymphoma. Imaging and tissue diagnosis guide treatment.'),
('Which paraneoplastic syndrome is typical of small cell lung cancer?',
 'SIADH', ['PTHrP hypercalcaemia', 'Polycythaemia', 'Hypoglycaemia'],
 'Small cell cancer secretes ADH and ACTH, causing hyponatraemia and ectopic Cushing syndrome. PTHrP hypercalcaemia is classic for squamous cell carcinoma.'),
('A man with joint pains has ferritin 1100 ug/l and transferrin saturation 62%. Next test?',
 'HFE genotyping', ['Serum caeruloplasmin', 'Bone marrow iron stain', 'Haemoglobin electrophoresis'],
 'High ferritin with transferrin saturation above 45% suggests hereditary haemochromatosis; C282Y homozygosity confirms it. Caeruloplasmin is the test for Wilson disease.'),
('First-line disease-modifying drug for newly diagnosed rheumatoid arthritis without contraindications:',
 'Methotrexate', ['Long-term prednisone', 'Allopurinol', 'Colchicine'],
 'EULAR recommends methotrexate as the anchor DMARD, often with short-term low-dose steroid as a bridge. Steroids alone do not stop joint damage.'),
('Joint aspirate from an acutely swollen first toe joint under polarised light would show:',
 'Negatively birefringent needles', ['Positively birefringent rhomboids', 'Flat cholesterol plates', 'Calcium oxalate envelopes'],
 'Urate crystals in gout are needle-shaped and negatively birefringent. Positively birefringent rhomboid crystals are calcium pyrophosphate (pseudogout).'),
('Which antibody is typical of drug-induced lupus, for example after hydralazine?',
 'Anti-histone', ['Anti-Sm', 'Anti-Scl-70', 'Anti-Jo-1'],
 'Anti-histone antibodies are found in most drug-induced lupus, which usually spares the kidneys and resolves when the drug stops. Anti-Scl-70 marks diffuse systemic sclerosis and anti-Jo-1 myositis.'),
('Under the ESC 2024 guideline, what office systolic target suits most treated adults who tolerate it?',
 '120 to 129 mmHg', ['Below 150 mmHg', 'Below 110 mmHg', '140 to 149 mmHg'],
 'ESC 2024 recommends aiming for 120 to 129 mmHg systolic if tolerated, with a more lenient target in frailty or symptomatic hypotension.'),
('Which is a major Duke criterion for infective endocarditis?',
 'Typical organism in two cultures', ['Fever above 38 C', 'Prosthetic heart valve', 'Janeway lesions on palms'],
 'Typical organisms in two separate blood cultures, or echocardiographic evidence of endocardial involvement, are major criteria. Fever, predisposition and vascular signs are minor.'),
('An 82-year-old at high surgical risk has severe symptomatic aortic stenosis. Preferred treatment?',
 'TAVI', ['Long-term nitrates', 'Watchful waiting', 'Mitral valve repair'],
 'ESC/EACTS guidance favours transcatheter aortic valve implantation for older or high-risk patients with severe symptomatic stenosis. Nitrates can cause dangerous hypotension in aortic stenosis.'),
]
